s it  wasn't enough and I think people didn't  really understand the full depth of the  problem of safeguarding the cultural  problem that you had within Oxfam the  there was a very fundamental problem  here which needed more fundament  measures to treat it we are now doing  that we are now we've tripled the amount  of money we're spending on safeguarding  we've got a hundred investigators  trained based in every country which we  work in around the world we've put every  single member of staff through online  safeguarding training we are beginning  to make the moves now at the sort of  level and scale which I think will make  a difference but unfortunately that  wasn't done earlier but where are you  okay are taking that on board where are  you then in this process that the  Charity Commission describes so that you  need further cultural and systemic  change you saying that actually has  occurred or now they all got some way to  go we've still got somewhere to go as  cultural and they are right to say we  need cultural and systemic change that  is a very difficult thing to deliver it  means putting some a good solid  foundation there and so implementing the  recommendations they've made 79  recommendations we've already  implemented well over 2/3 of them that  will help give us the foundation and the  base from which to change but the real  change we need to make and why I'm  particularly pleased that we've had put  so many members of staff through  safeguarding training is we need to stop  to get a culture which feels that  somehow safeguarding is something that's  done by directors of safeguarding the  safeguarding is something that's that's  for investigation and understand that  it's about creating the right culture  and environment in which people can work  and it's the responsibility of every  person who works in Oxfam whether there  are a trustee or a receptionist or a an  aid worker out and out in the field  everyone needs to be working to create  the right environment of transparency of  safety and safety is not just about  stopping these things occurring but  crucially about creating an environment  where people feel they can report them  it's a huge bureaucratic operation then  isn't it you mentioned there the huge  number of extra staff you're going to  have to recruit to to oversee this and  ultimately that means with with a  shrinking pot I know that less money is  going to go on aids in its it does  require more money and I think one of  the things we need to do and we already  started doing is having conversations  with donors particular with  institutional donors about how we make  sure that we have enough funds from the  money they give them to ensure that we  work in the right  sort of safe environment and can deliver  the safety that everyone is but as a  proportion of I don't know what what it  is it's a proportion of the funds you  get in and how much is spent on the  front line which is what all donors want  to see in all child ease isn't it as  much as possible where it's going to be  most effective some of that has to be  diverted then now does it not we've have  a target of only deliver only spending  ten percent of our income on overheads  and on administration it may be that  that has to extend to 11 or 12 percent  in order to account to encourage and and  make sure that we deliver on this I  think everyone would agree that that  would be money really well spent  because creating the safe environment  for our beneficiaries and for our staff  is really absolutely crucial and what  are your concerns about the fact that  Haiti may not be alone incident has  already been discussion about Oxfam and  similar types of activities in Chad now  given the the scores for one imagines  that the hundreds of countries that  Oxfam has operated in over the decades  you've got a huge investigative task  Avenue we've just trained the hundred  investigators who are out there in all  our countries where we're operating